WEBVTT

00:00:00.241 --> 00:00:02.996
Last time, potassium burst into a lilac flame

00:00:03.137 --> 00:00:03.600
on water.

00:00:04.303 --> 00:00:06.915
Now put a drop of potassium chloride solution

00:00:07.037 --> 00:00:08.101
into a Bunsen flame.

00:00:08.725 --> 00:00:10.993
The sample isn't burning, but the flame turns

00:00:11.094 --> 00:00:11.475
lilac.

00:00:12.159 --> 00:00:14.086
Sodium compounds turn it yellow-orange.

00:00:14.690 --> 00:00:16.306
So the colour belongs to the metal.

00:00:17.054 --> 00:00:18.928
Chemists use this in a flame test.

00:00:19.413 --> 00:00:21.200
But how much can a flame colour really

00:00:21.261 --> 00:00:24.133
tell you, and what can't it identify reliably?

00:00:25.082 --> 00:00:27.352
You've met the cause already, with hydrogen's line

00:00:27.392 --> 00:00:27.814
spectrum.

00:00:28.416 --> 00:00:30.782
The flame's heat gives the metal's electrons energy,

00:00:31.123 --> 00:00:32.668
lifting them to higher energy levels.

00:00:33.269 --> 00:00:35.154
As they drop back, they give out photons,

00:00:35.415 --> 00:00:37.520
with energies set by the gaps between that

00:00:37.582 --> 00:00:38.584
metal's own levels.

00:00:39.306 --> 00:00:40.947
A flame shows several of these at once,

00:00:41.328 --> 00:00:43.049
and your eye blends them into a single

00:00:43.090 --> 00:00:43.390
colour.

00:00:43.893 --> 00:00:45.802
So each metal gives its own colour.

00:00:46.740 --> 00:00:47.871
Here's how the practical runs.

00:00:48.497 --> 00:00:50.906
It follows your school's risk assessment, with solutions

00:00:50.927 --> 00:00:53.496
your teacher has approved, usually metal chlorides.

00:00:54.199 --> 00:00:55.880
You'll need a Bunsen burner with the air

00:00:55.921 --> 00:00:58.381
hole open, giving a hot blue flame, and

00:00:58.422 --> 00:01:00.703
a nichrome wire loop, which doesn't colour the

00:01:00.724 --> 00:01:01.364
flame itself.

00:01:02.247 --> 00:01:04.054
Clean the loop as your school's method says,

00:01:04.517 --> 00:01:06.425
then heat it until the flame shows no

00:01:06.506 --> 00:01:06.766
colour.

00:01:07.490 --> 00:01:09.739
Then dip the loop in a solution, hold

00:01:09.780 --> 00:01:11.004
it at the edge of the blue flame,

00:01:11.346 --> 00:01:12.751
and record the colour straight away.

00:01:13.437 --> 00:01:15.048
Clean the loop, and repeat with the next

00:01:15.069 --> 00:01:15.431
solution.

00:01:16.157 --> 00:01:17.448
Each step has a reason.

00:01:18.094 --> 00:01:20.064
That first test, with a clean loop and

00:01:20.145 --> 00:01:21.612
no sample, is a blank.

00:01:22.256 --> 00:01:23.981
It shows what the loop and flame give

00:01:24.122 --> 00:01:24.723
on their own,

00:01:25.246 --> 00:01:27.171
so any colour you see later comes from

00:01:27.212 --> 00:01:27.713
the sample.

00:01:28.536 --> 00:01:31.180
A blue flame matters, because the yellow flame

00:01:31.221 --> 00:01:33.083
you get with the air hole closed would

00:01:33.144 --> 00:01:33.884
hide the colours.

00:01:34.787 --> 00:01:37.781
Cleaning between tests stops one sample colouring the

00:01:37.822 --> 00:01:38.063
next.

00:01:38.787 --> 00:01:41.212
And testing each solution more than once checks

00:01:41.253 --> 00:01:42.896
that your observation is reliable.

00:01:43.579 --> 00:01:46.468
For safety, wear safety glasses, tie back long

00:01:46.509 --> 00:01:48.735
hair, and remember the loop stays hot.

00:01:49.359 --> 00:01:51.410
Some of these compounds are toxic, so wash

00:01:51.431 --> 00:01:52.375
your hands afterwards.

00:01:53.279 --> 00:01:54.433
Here's what you'll record.

00:01:55.298 --> 00:01:57.043
Lithium gives a crimson red.

00:01:57.727 --> 00:01:59.694
Sodium, a bright yellow-orange.

00:02:00.398 --> 00:02:02.064
Potassium, a pale lilac.

00:02:02.728 --> 00:02:04.214
Calcium, an orange-red.

00:02:04.879 --> 00:02:07.427
Strontium, a deep red, a lot like lithium's.

00:02:08.071 --> 00:02:09.466
Barium, a pale green.

00:02:09.951 --> 00:02:11.458
And copper, a blue-green.

00:02:12.323 --> 00:02:13.916
So what does a colour tell you?

00:02:14.661 --> 00:02:16.488
It tells you which metal is likely to

00:02:16.529 --> 00:02:18.577
be present, because the colour comes from the

00:02:18.657 --> 00:02:19.601
metal's electrons.

00:02:20.324 --> 00:02:21.310
It doesn't tell you the rest of the

00:02:21.350 --> 00:02:21.792
compound.

00:02:22.374 --> 00:02:25.181
Sodium chloride and sodium nitrate both give the

00:02:25.221 --> 00:02:26.824
same yellow-orange flame.

00:02:27.486 --> 00:02:29.633
The chloride or nitrate part doesn't change the

00:02:29.693 --> 00:02:29.953
colour.

00:02:30.857 --> 00:02:32.862
And because you watch and record it yourself,

00:02:33.325 --> 00:02:34.327
it's primary data.

00:02:34.936 --> 00:02:35.561
Quick prediction.

00:02:36.347 --> 00:02:39.139
An unknown solution gives a crimson-red flame.

00:02:39.988 --> 00:02:40.637
Which metal is it?

00:02:41.449 --> 00:02:42.943
Think about the colours we just recorded.

00:02:43.816 --> 00:02:44.202
Have a think.

00:02:48.183 --> 00:02:49.311
If you said lithium,

00:02:49.815 --> 00:02:50.681
you might be right.

00:02:51.043 --> 00:02:53.402
But strontium gives a very similar red.

00:02:54.067 --> 00:02:56.505
By eye, you can't reliably tell them apart.

00:02:57.269 --> 00:02:59.278
Calcium's orange-red is close as well.

00:03:00.143 --> 00:03:01.800
Colours are hard to describe, too.

00:03:02.566 --> 00:03:05.265
One person's crimson is another person's scarlet.

00:03:05.969 --> 00:03:06.168
So a

00:03:06.691 --> 00:03:08.957
colour alone can point to more than one

00:03:08.998 --> 00:03:09.238
metal.

00:03:10.600 --> 00:03:11.629
Here's the second problem.

00:03:12.415 --> 00:03:14.756
Sodium compounds are almost everywhere,

00:03:15.338 --> 00:03:18.220
in dust, on skin, in tap water, even

00:03:18.241 --> 00:03:18.900
on glassware.

00:03:19.582 --> 00:03:22.132
And sodium's yellow-orange is very bright.

00:03:22.915 --> 00:03:24.935
So a tiny trace of sodium can swamp

00:03:24.956 --> 00:03:28.018
a weaker colour, like potassium's pale lilac.

00:03:28.761 --> 00:03:31.217
In a mixture, the strongest colour tends to

00:03:31.239 --> 00:03:31.399
win.

00:03:32.224 --> 00:03:34.270
That's why the blank, and a clean loop,

00:03:34.592 --> 00:03:35.213
really matter.

00:03:35.928 --> 00:03:37.856
Some metals give no useful colour at all.

00:03:38.358 --> 00:03:41.966
Magnesium compounds, for example, give out mostly ultraviolet

00:03:42.007 --> 00:03:43.430
light, which you can't see.

00:03:44.013 --> 00:03:45.058
The flame just stays blue.

00:03:45.784 --> 00:03:47.975
And a flame test says nothing about how

00:03:48.157 --> 00:03:49.322
much of a metal is there.

00:03:49.946 --> 00:03:52.253
A faint colour might mean a dilute solution,

00:03:52.475 --> 00:03:53.397
or just a poor dip.

00:03:54.237 --> 00:03:56.430
So how do chemists tell lithium from strontium?

00:03:57.175 --> 00:03:59.198
They spread the flame's light out with a

00:03:59.299 --> 00:04:02.703
spectroscope, a small instrument that separates light into

00:04:02.744 --> 00:04:04.687
its colours, like the prism we used for

00:04:04.708 --> 00:04:05.127
hydrogen.

00:04:06.053 --> 00:04:07.684
Then you see the actual lines.

00:04:08.491 --> 00:04:10.866
Lithium shows one strong red line.

00:04:11.531 --> 00:04:14.359
Strontium shows several red and orange bands, and

00:04:14.380 --> 00:04:14.920
a blue line.

00:04:15.664 --> 00:04:18.493
Each metal's pattern is its fingerprint, so the

00:04:18.555 --> 00:04:20.541
line pattern can identify a metal that the

00:04:20.582 --> 00:04:21.103
colour can't.

00:04:21.991 --> 00:04:23.444
So here's the rule to keep.

00:04:24.131 --> 00:04:26.198
A flame colour is a clue, and the

00:04:26.259 --> 00:04:27.764
line pattern is the evidence.

00:04:28.428 --> 00:04:29.251
Colour suggests,

00:04:29.855 --> 00:04:31.019
spectrum confirms.

00:04:33.922 --> 00:04:34.346
Your turn.

00:04:35.091 --> 00:04:37.519
A student carries out flame tests on two

00:04:37.740 --> 00:04:38.763
unlabelled solutions.

00:04:39.525 --> 00:04:41.327
Solution A gives a crimson flame,

00:04:41.868 --> 00:04:43.970
and solution B gives a lilac flame.

00:04:44.813 --> 00:04:47.467
Identify the metal ions that could be present

00:04:47.627 --> 00:04:47.909
in each.

00:04:48.853 --> 00:04:51.296
Explain why the result for A isn't certain,

00:04:51.816 --> 00:04:53.258
and suggest a way to confirm it.

00:04:54.104 --> 00:04:54.895
Pause here to answer.

00:05:05.462 --> 00:05:07.278
Solution B is potassium.

00:05:07.762 --> 00:05:10.845
Solution A could be lithium or strontium, because

00:05:10.925 --> 00:05:12.948
both give a very similar crimson red.

00:05:13.669 --> 00:05:16.476
To confirm, view the flame through a spectroscope

00:05:16.817 --> 00:05:18.722
and compare its lines with known samples.

00:05:19.605 --> 00:05:21.905
Writing just lithium for A is the slip.

00:05:22.350 --> 00:05:23.678
The colour can't separate the two.

00:05:25.302 --> 00:05:25.725
Last one.

00:05:26.691 --> 00:05:29.519
A student flame tests a solution of potassium

00:05:29.579 --> 00:05:32.848
chloride, but sees a yellow-orange flame instead of

00:05:32.908 --> 00:05:33.329
lilac.

00:05:34.132 --> 00:05:36.879
Identify the most likely cause, and explain why

00:05:36.939 --> 00:05:39.946
the student shouldn't conclude that potassium is absent.

00:05:40.669 --> 00:05:41.537
Pause here to answer.

00:05:52.195 --> 00:05:54.423
The likely cause is sodium contamination,

00:05:54.946 --> 00:05:56.672
from a dirty loop or glassware.

00:05:57.355 --> 00:06:01.649
Sodium's bright yellow-orange swamps potassium's pale lilac, so

00:06:01.691 --> 00:06:03.556
the potassium can be there but hidden.

00:06:04.339 --> 00:06:06.363
Clean the loop until the blank shows no

00:06:06.424 --> 00:06:07.687
colour, and test again.

00:06:08.349 --> 00:06:10.637
Concluding that potassium is absent is the trap.

00:06:12.849 --> 00:06:15.659
Everything in today's video happened outside the nucleus,

00:06:16.001 --> 00:06:18.008
as electrons moved between energy levels.

00:06:18.531 --> 00:06:21.913
But some atoms change inside the nucleus itself,

00:06:22.554 --> 00:06:23.775
and give out radiation.

00:06:24.416 --> 00:06:27.677
So what radiation do unstable isotopes give off,

00:06:28.179 --> 00:06:30.299
and does it matter whether they're natural or

00:06:30.380 --> 00:06:30.920
human-made?

00:06:33.595 --> 00:06:34.417
Here are the key notes.

00:06:34.941 --> 00:06:36.488
Pause the video if you need more time

00:06:36.529 --> 00:06:36.950
to copy them.
